WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.100
You know, I want you to try something for a second.

00:03.020 --> 00:08.620
Think back to a really crucial, vivid memory from a few years ago.

00:08.720 --> 00:09.080
Oh, sure.

00:09.220 --> 00:16.860
Like maybe it's a conversation that completely changed your life trajectory or, I don't know, a sudden realization you had while you were traveling.

00:16.920 --> 00:17.220
Right.

00:17.540 --> 00:21.060
When you try to pull up that exact detail, what actually happens?

00:21.580 --> 00:28.300
You realize pretty quickly that your brain didn't store like a perfect high definition video recording of the event.

00:28.400 --> 00:29.060
No, not at all.

00:29.060 --> 00:38.080
Instead, what you get is a feeling, a vague impression, a reconstruction that actually shifts slightly depending on, you know, what mood you're in today.

00:38.180 --> 00:38.620
Exactly.

00:38.760 --> 00:38.960
Yeah.

00:39.200 --> 00:45.220
And we are so conditioned to think of our brains as faulty hard drives like, oh, I forgot the exact phrasing of what she said.

00:45.240 --> 00:46.520
My memory is so flawed.

00:46.520 --> 00:55.820
But what if that fuzziness, that exact lack of a perfect recording is actually the foundational reason why you are smarter than Chats GPT?

00:55.980 --> 00:57.840
It totally flips the script, doesn't it?

00:57.900 --> 00:58.780
It really does.

00:58.780 --> 00:59.000
It really does.

00:59.060 --> 01:05.900
I mean, we constantly judge ourselves against the standard of a machine, just assuming a perfect digital record is the absolute pinnacle of memory.

01:06.840 --> 01:11.560
But biology isn't trying to be a database and failing.

01:11.800 --> 01:13.400
It's doing something entirely different.

01:13.540 --> 01:13.780
Right.

01:13.780 --> 01:19.460
And as we are starting to realize, it's doing something vastly more sophisticated than just storing data.

01:19.560 --> 01:20.000
Exactly.

01:20.620 --> 01:24.840
And that brings us to the core mystery we are tearing apart today.

01:24.840 --> 01:35.840
We're looking at the fundamental illusion of artificial intelligence and why current AI might be, well, structurally, mathematically incapable of ever actually experiencing the world the way we do.

01:35.980 --> 01:36.820
It's a huge claim.

01:36.960 --> 01:37.420
Huge.

01:37.420 --> 01:43.780
And our map for this deep dive is a truly fascinating, incredibly dense theoretical paper by the author, Flyxion.

01:43.780 --> 01:49.020
It's titled, Histories Become Operators, Biological Notes on Retained Admissibility.

01:49.120 --> 01:49.560
Really awful.

01:49.560 --> 01:50.820
Yeah, but so good.

01:50.820 --> 01:58.660
This essay analyzes a deeply profound public conversation between two brilliant minds, scientists Michael Levin and Katrina Schleisman.

01:59.040 --> 02:01.100
They tackle memory, agency, and learning.

02:01.100 --> 02:08.060
And what makes Flyxion's paper so vital right now is that it isn't just a philosophical summary of their chat.

02:08.220 --> 02:09.180
It's an extraction.

02:09.800 --> 02:09.940
Right.

02:10.120 --> 02:10.300
Yeah.

02:10.480 --> 02:19.100
It pulls out these highly precise claims about how living things remember and adapt, and it plugs them into a formal mathematical framework.

02:19.320 --> 02:24.720
It essentially creates a blueprint for understanding the gap between a biological mind and a silicon one.

02:24.720 --> 02:38.360
Okay, let's unpack this, because if we're going to understand why today's AI is fundamentally stuck and how biology elegantly solves the problem AI is currently facing, we have to start by completely dismantling our assumptions.

02:38.580 --> 02:39.100
Oh, absolutely.

02:39.280 --> 02:50.800
We basically have to unlearn the standard engineering definition of memory, because, let's face it, the engineering habit is so deeply ingrained in us that we don't even realize we're using it as a metaphor for our own brains.

02:50.840 --> 02:51.380
We really do.

02:51.380 --> 02:55.520
I mean, we talk about processing information, storing memories, retrieving facts.

02:55.740 --> 02:56.380
Like, we're computers.

02:56.720 --> 02:56.920
Right.

02:57.220 --> 03:01.900
But the traditional engineering model of memory is what Flyxion calls a fidelity problem.

03:02.580 --> 03:05.340
Let's think about how a computer hard drive actually works.

03:06.120 --> 03:08.900
You write a bit of data, a one or a zero.

03:09.060 --> 03:16.300
The entire existential task of that computer system is to protect that exact bit from drifting.

03:16.300 --> 03:22.820
Right, because if a one becomes a zero, your software crashes, or, I don't know, your bank account suddenly has a lot less money in it.

03:23.200 --> 03:23.560
Exactly.

03:24.140 --> 03:34.380
So engineers use redundancy, they use error correction codes, all to ensure that when you go to read that data a year later, you get the exact same bit back, perfect fidelity.

03:34.580 --> 03:34.680
Right.

03:34.860 --> 03:37.500
But here is the critical part that we often overlook.

03:38.140 --> 03:44.460
In that engineering model, the meaning of the bit lives completely outside the storage medium.

03:44.460 --> 03:46.120
Wait, what do you mean outside?

03:46.480 --> 03:49.940
Meaning is assigned by the outside observer, the human user who reads it.

03:50.300 --> 03:53.120
The hard drive doesn't know what a photo of your dog means, it doesn't care.

03:53.380 --> 03:57.280
It just stores the pixels with perfect fidelity so you can derive meaning from it later.

03:57.460 --> 04:00.280
Okay, so a hard drive is basically a perfect dumb vault.

04:00.460 --> 04:00.840
Exactly.

04:00.840 --> 04:05.640
But Michael Levin proposes a counter-premise for biology that is just mind-blowing.

04:06.180 --> 04:10.840
He says a living system never actually has access to its own past, not directly.

04:11.180 --> 04:12.240
It doesn't have a vault.

04:12.520 --> 04:15.480
What it has instead are memory traces.

04:15.800 --> 04:18.520
He describes them as messages from your past self.

04:18.520 --> 04:22.420
Yes, and the reason for this is purely physical.

04:23.680 --> 04:30.520
Biological substrates, the actual wetware of our brains, our cells, our proteins, they are inherently unreliable.

04:31.320 --> 04:32.200
Because they're messy.

04:32.380 --> 04:37.300
They are messy, they degrade, they drift, the temperature changes, the chemical gradients fluctuate constantly.

04:37.660 --> 04:42.140
So evolution couldn't optimize for perfect, unchanging storage like a hard drive.

04:42.380 --> 04:46.080
That would be a losing battle against the basic physics of biology.

04:46.080 --> 04:49.340
Wait, so instead of fighting the messiness, evolution leaned into it.

04:49.520 --> 04:49.920
Precisely.

04:50.420 --> 04:55.320
Because evolution couldn't guarantee perfect storage, it optimized for something else entirely.

04:55.780 --> 04:57.080
The creativity of the reading.

04:57.260 --> 04:57.760
Oh, wow.

04:57.980 --> 05:05.960
Biological memory preserves salience, meaning what actually matters, what is important for survival and adaptation, rather than perfect physical accuracy.

05:06.060 --> 05:07.260
I'm trying to visualize this.

05:07.320 --> 05:10.620
So it's not like opening a perfectly preserved PDF on your desktop.

05:10.620 --> 05:17.000
It feels more like, imagine finding a cryptic diary entry left by a past version of yourself from 10 years ago.

05:17.100 --> 05:18.600
That's a great way to think about it.

05:18.820 --> 05:19.100
Right.

05:19.240 --> 05:23.100
You don't just passively read it like a computer fetching a file.

05:23.220 --> 05:24.740
You have to actively interpret it.

05:24.800 --> 05:25.180
Right, yeah.

05:25.380 --> 05:27.380
The words might say, I can't believe they did that.

05:27.480 --> 05:34.520
And you have to sit there and reconstruct the meaning based on who you are now, what you know now, the context of your current life.

05:34.740 --> 05:36.780
You're reading it through the lens of the present.

05:37.060 --> 05:39.520
You are actively recreating the memory.

05:39.520 --> 05:41.440
And the act of recreation is the key.

05:41.980 --> 05:46.240
The organism is its own interpreter from the very first moment.

05:46.980 --> 05:48.360
Evolution didn't build a vault.

05:48.820 --> 05:51.700
It built an incredibly sophisticated interpretive engine.

05:52.000 --> 05:53.240
Salience over fidelity.

05:53.580 --> 05:53.860
Yes.

05:54.020 --> 06:03.900
But I want to push on this because it's easy to just say biology interprets things, but the paper brings up a concrete example from developmental biology that really makes this unavoidable.

06:03.900 --> 06:12.680
It compares standard embryogenesis, how an embryo normally develops, with the induced formation of these things called anthrobots.

06:12.780 --> 06:14.320
Oh, the anthrobots are fascinating.

06:14.480 --> 06:15.620
Can we really dig into this?

06:15.700 --> 06:19.400
Because this example completely shattered my understanding of how DNA works.

06:19.400 --> 06:23.760
It absolutely breaks the standard engineering metaphor of DNA.

06:24.300 --> 06:27.900
So let's look at the work coming out of Michael Levin's lab.

06:28.560 --> 06:37.140
Normally, if you take cells from a frog embryo specifically, the cells that are destined to become the skin of the frog, they know what to do.

06:37.200 --> 06:37.360
Right.

06:37.480 --> 06:38.120
They become skin.

06:38.420 --> 06:38.780
Exactly.

06:38.940 --> 06:43.180
They form a passive barrier on the outside of the tadpole to keep bacteria out.

06:43.180 --> 06:45.820
That is their quote-unquote programmed job.

06:46.220 --> 06:46.240
Right.

06:46.520 --> 06:47.920
Standard embryogenesis.

06:48.080 --> 06:50.040
But Levin's team did something incredible.

06:50.720 --> 06:56.820
They scraped some of those skin cells off the embryo, dissociated them, and put them in a novel environment.

06:56.900 --> 06:57.880
Like a Petri dish.

06:58.060 --> 06:58.840
Yeah, a Petri dish.

06:58.960 --> 06:59.100
Yeah.

06:59.160 --> 07:01.500
Completely isolated from the rest of the embryo.

07:01.840 --> 07:05.400
No muscle cells, no nerve cells, just skin cells alone together.

07:05.580 --> 07:11.540
Okay, so going by the engineering mindset, if DNA is a blueprint, these cells should just sit there, right?

07:11.540 --> 07:18.240
Maybe form a flat layer of skin in the dish, or just die because they are missing the rest of the blueprint's contact.

07:18.300 --> 07:18.740
Exactly.

07:18.900 --> 07:19.720
That's what you'd expect.

07:19.860 --> 07:21.140
But that is not what happens.

07:21.320 --> 07:27.200
Instead of forming a flat passive layer, these cells get together and reboot their multicellularity.

07:27.300 --> 07:27.560
Reboot?

07:27.700 --> 07:34.820
Yeah, they form entirely new independent living structures called xenobots or anthrobots if they're derived from human cells.

07:35.120 --> 07:36.880
They form a spherical body.

07:37.060 --> 07:37.520
Whoa.

07:37.520 --> 07:46.020
And they take the little hairs, called cilia, which normally just move mucus around on the frog skin, and they use them as oars to swim around the dish.

07:46.120 --> 07:54.980
They can navigate mazes, they can spontaneously sweep up loose material into piles, and if you cut them in half, they heal themselves.

07:55.220 --> 08:00.700
And just to be completely clear, they have the exact same DNA as the normal frog skin cells.

08:01.060 --> 08:03.120
Materially identical genetic information.

08:03.220 --> 08:03.400
Yeah.

08:03.740 --> 08:06.020
Not a single letter of the DNA was altered.

08:06.020 --> 08:07.440
That is wild.

08:07.560 --> 08:07.940
Isn't it?

08:08.080 --> 08:09.560
The genome didn't change at all.

08:09.760 --> 08:14.520
So if I give builders a blueprint, they build the exact same house every time, no matter where they are.

08:14.700 --> 08:22.340
But here, the same genetic blueprint built a flat skin barrier one minute, and a swimming, navigating, healing robot the next.

08:22.440 --> 08:24.060
Which means it isn't a blueprint at all.

08:24.380 --> 08:25.880
The genome is a trace.

08:26.180 --> 08:26.980
It's a constraint.

08:26.980 --> 08:31.920
What changed was the interpretive context that the trace was being read in.

08:32.600 --> 08:39.940
The cells found themselves in a new environment, and they creatively interpreted their genetic traces to solve a new problem.

08:40.520 --> 08:43.660
How to survive as a completely novel organism.

08:44.040 --> 08:49.380
We are talking about thousands of genes being expressed entirely differently between the two outcomes.

08:49.380 --> 08:55.380
So it feels less like a strict architectural blueprint, and more like sheet music.

08:55.740 --> 08:56.400
Ooh, I like that.

08:56.480 --> 08:57.360
Like a jazz standard.

08:57.740 --> 09:03.460
You can hand the exact same sheet music for autumn leaves to a classical symphony and a five-piece jazz band.

09:03.720 --> 09:06.860
The notes on the page, the constraints, the DNA are identical.

09:07.120 --> 09:07.400
Right.

09:07.400 --> 09:12.640
But because the context is different, because the instruments and the environment are different, you get two completely different songs.

09:13.260 --> 09:16.120
And crucially, neither is the wrong way to play the notes.

09:16.380 --> 09:20.420
They are both entirely coherent expressions of the same underlying trace.

09:20.600 --> 09:21.760
That's a brilliant analogy.

09:22.200 --> 09:23.660
The environment is the orchestra.

09:24.200 --> 09:28.980
The constraint is there, but the interpretation by the living agent creates the final form.

09:28.980 --> 09:40.780
Okay, so if biological memory, whether it's DNA or our own memories of childhood, is this active, creative interpretation of traces by an agent living in a specific context,

09:41.400 --> 09:46.740
what happens when a system has absolutely no traces of its own lived experience to interpret?

09:47.060 --> 09:52.240
And this brings us right to the doorstep of Katrina Schleisman's critique of artificial intelligence.

09:52.300 --> 09:53.680
Yes, this part is huge.

09:53.680 --> 10:01.060
Because she looks at current large language models, the AIs we chat with every single day, and points out a massive structural deficit.

10:01.360 --> 10:09.780
Right, because if you're using CAT-GPT right now, you might be thinking, wait, I upload my past chat logs, it remembers what I said yesterday, so my AI definitely has a memory.

10:10.240 --> 10:13.800
But Schleisman draws a hard line between two types of memory, right?

10:14.260 --> 10:15.260
Episodic and semantic.

10:15.460 --> 10:16.240
Let's break those down.

10:16.660 --> 10:18.640
Schleisman differentiates them beautifully.

10:19.180 --> 10:24.300
Episodic memory is storing and creatively reconstructing specific lived experiences.

10:24.460 --> 10:25.580
Like an episode of a TV show.

10:25.680 --> 10:26.440
Yeah, exactly.

10:26.800 --> 10:33.040
It's remembering your 10th birthday party, the smell of the cake, the specific feeling of excitement who is sitting next to you.

10:33.240 --> 10:35.120
It is anchored in time and space to you.

10:35.440 --> 10:39.760
Semantic memory, on the other hand, is the abstraction of general facts from those experiences.

10:40.320 --> 10:43.640
It's knowing the general fact that humans celebrate birthdays with cake.

10:43.640 --> 10:49.400
So, episodic is the movie of your life, and semantic is the Wikipedia article about life in general.

10:49.540 --> 10:49.860
Exactly.

10:50.220 --> 10:54.480
And her central point is that LLMs are essentially pure semantic memory.

10:55.200 --> 11:00.120
They are a massively compressed, decontextualized store of general knowledge.

11:00.220 --> 11:02.720
They have all the facts, but no life.

11:02.900 --> 11:03.140
Right.

11:03.340 --> 11:04.660
Here's where it gets really interesting.

11:04.660 --> 11:06.260
They have no episodic layer.

11:06.680 --> 11:13.960
An LLM has no actual record of what happened to it, when it happened, or under what specific lived conditions it learned a particular fact.

11:14.520 --> 11:19.460
As Flyxion writes, AI is the limit case of memory missing its biological half.

11:19.740 --> 11:22.020
It's all general facts, zero lived experience.

11:22.300 --> 11:27.720
Biological memory relies on traces that must be interpreted in the present moment by an agent with a continuous existence.

11:27.860 --> 11:29.400
Right, an agent that's actually there.

11:29.400 --> 11:32.800
And current large models don't have those traces.

11:33.460 --> 11:38.340
They just have the mathematical residue of their massive training data compressed into weights.

11:38.780 --> 11:43.680
And those weights were never indexed to a particular lived episode in the first place.

11:44.160 --> 11:46.820
The AI didn't, you know, experience reading the internet.

11:47.260 --> 11:50.420
Data was just pushed through its architecture to update its parameters.

11:50.420 --> 11:58.040
But wait, let me play devil's advocate here, because the AI industry is completely obsessed with fixing this exact problem right now.

11:58.040 --> 12:01.880
What about things like RAG retrieval augmented generation?

12:02.120 --> 12:02.760
Ah, yes.

12:03.040 --> 12:11.100
Developers are constantly feeding an AI its past chat logs or giving it access to a personal database of your conversation, specifically so it remembers your history.

12:11.640 --> 12:15.460
Doesn't giving it a database of its past chats give it an episodic memory?

12:15.460 --> 12:17.040
It's a great question.

12:17.780 --> 12:21.280
And the paper explicitly pushes back hard on this industry trend.

12:22.040 --> 12:31.780
Appending conversational history into a retrieval store, like a vector database that the AI searches before it replies to you, is treating the symptom, not the underlying structure.

12:31.880 --> 12:40.780
When you dump old chat logs into a database for the AI to search, you are just reintroducing that old engineering storage style fidelity.

12:41.300 --> 12:45.100
You're just giving a giant semantic calculator a hard drive to search.

12:45.100 --> 12:46.680
Because it's still just fetching data.

12:46.860 --> 12:48.580
It's not experiencing the recall.

12:48.740 --> 12:49.120
Exactly.

12:49.520 --> 12:53.640
The missing ingredient in AI was never perfect fidelity or lack of data access.

12:54.320 --> 12:57.920
The missing ingredient, as the paper points out, is indexicality.

12:58.440 --> 12:58.960
Indexicality.

12:59.060 --> 12:59.240
Okay.

12:59.660 --> 13:04.160
Meaning tying a memory to a specific, uniquely lived past occasion.

13:04.380 --> 13:04.640
Yes.

13:04.640 --> 13:15.680
It's the difference between handing someone a printed transcript of a conversation they had yesterday versus them actually having the internal subjective memory of feeling that conversation happened.

13:15.800 --> 13:19.380
Reading a transcript of your own life isn't the same as remembering it.

13:19.500 --> 13:20.040
Precisely.

13:20.320 --> 13:33.580
Which is why Schleisman and her colleague Eric Davis proposed that if we truly want autonomous agents, artificial systems that can actually act as teammates rather than just encyclopedias, we have to stop treating episodic recall as a tacked-on database search.

13:33.580 --> 13:35.120
We need a structural change.

13:35.440 --> 13:35.780
Exactly.

13:36.260 --> 13:49.400
We have to structurally integrate episodic and semantic memory directly within the agent itself so that interpreting its own specific history becomes part of how the system physically processes the present moment.

13:49.400 --> 13:59.720
Okay. So, if true biological memory isn't just a database lookup, and if our past isn't a strict blueprint telling us exactly what to do, how does our past guide our future?

14:00.140 --> 14:05.420
If the frog DNA doesn't force the cells to become flat-skinned, what is it actually doing?

14:05.740 --> 14:08.400
This is where we move into the paper's second major concept.

14:09.420 --> 14:11.060
Constraint is not determination.

14:11.580 --> 14:11.920
Okay.

14:12.160 --> 14:15.340
And this is where we get the core conceptual equation of Flyxion's paper.

14:15.400 --> 14:18.120
We promise no dry math reading, but let's talk about the concept.

14:18.120 --> 14:21.300
The paper frames it as history becoming an operator.

14:21.580 --> 14:24.400
Yes. History crystallized into operative constraint.

14:24.820 --> 14:25.920
HT goes to FT.

14:26.260 --> 14:26.380
Right.

14:26.820 --> 14:33.680
What that means is that the history of a system, whether that's millions of years of evolution shaping a genome or the past 10 years of your personal memories,

14:34.240 --> 14:37.500
acts as a geometric shape that narrows down the space of possible futures.

14:38.020 --> 14:40.280
The paper calls this continuation geometry.

14:40.560 --> 14:41.580
Continuation geometry.

14:41.580 --> 14:42.220
Yeah.

14:42.740 --> 14:48.340
The history defines what is mathematically admissible or possible for the system to do next.

14:48.600 --> 14:48.860
Okay.

14:48.940 --> 14:51.100
Let's visualize this continuation geometry.

14:51.760 --> 14:55.000
Think of it like you are standing at the entrance of a long hallway.

14:55.680 --> 14:59.900
The walls of that hallway represent your history, your memories, your genome.

15:00.420 --> 15:05.580
Those walls absolutely prevent you from moving left or right through solid drywall.

15:05.960 --> 15:08.380
You are heavily constrained by your past.

15:08.500 --> 15:09.220
Very heavily.

15:09.220 --> 15:14.780
But, and this is the crucial distinction, the walls do not dictate exactly how fast you walk forward.

15:15.100 --> 15:19.560
They don't tell you whether you skip or crawl or walk backwards or do cartwheels down the hall.

15:19.940 --> 15:25.600
You are mathematically constrained by the walls, but your exact path forward is not uniquely determined.

15:26.060 --> 15:28.080
That is a perfect visualization.

15:28.260 --> 15:28.360
Yeah.

15:28.400 --> 15:29.600
Constraint without determination.

15:30.100 --> 15:33.900
And what's fascinating here is where Michael Levin injects a highly critical nuance.

15:33.900 --> 15:42.840
He argues that this narrowing down the building of those hallway walls isn't just a one-time objective filter that gets stamped onto the system from the outside.

15:43.120 --> 15:43.160
Right.

15:43.340 --> 15:49.920
A constraint is actually redone uniquely by each continuer reading the trace under their own local conditions.

15:50.280 --> 15:51.920
Wait, I'm genuinely stuck here.

15:51.920 --> 16:00.460
If my history creates a constraint, if my past builds the walls of the hallway, how do I have any real agency?

16:00.800 --> 16:07.160
If the walls are already built by what happened to me yesterday, aren't my choices today already basically made for me?

16:07.800 --> 16:10.020
It sounds like determinism with extra steps.

16:10.020 --> 16:13.580
It would be if you were just a passive object bouncing down the hallway.

16:14.140 --> 16:17.700
But Levin is adamant that interpretation is a locus of agency.

16:18.460 --> 16:26.800
The continuer, the organism living in the present moment, is partly authoring what is admissible or possible in the very act of continuing.

16:27.340 --> 16:29.960
It's an act of reconstruction, not a passive filter.

16:30.120 --> 16:31.200
So the walls aren't fixed.

16:31.320 --> 16:32.940
Well, think back to the cryptic diary entry.

16:33.080 --> 16:34.580
The words on the page are the constraint.

16:34.920 --> 16:35.660
They don't change.

16:35.740 --> 16:35.960
Okay.

16:35.960 --> 16:41.240
But how those words constrain your behavior today depends entirely on how you actively interpret them right now.

16:41.900 --> 16:43.900
If it were a passive filter, the math would be easy.

16:44.340 --> 16:51.140
But because the biological system is actively interpreting its own constraint in real time, it makes the math incredibly difficult.

16:51.300 --> 16:51.800
I can imagine.

16:52.140 --> 16:57.140
Two systems can inherit the exact same history, the exact same traces, like the frog cells,

16:57.440 --> 17:03.900
and experience completely different admissible futures because the act of reading the constraint under different environmental conditions

17:03.900 --> 17:05.940
changes how the constraint applies.

17:05.960 --> 17:07.260
Okay, that clicks.

17:07.340 --> 17:14.620
It's like you and I both reading the exact same cryptic diary entry, but because I live in a dense city and you live off-grid in the woods,

17:14.820 --> 17:20.620
we interpret the advice totally differently and take completely different actions based on the exact same words.

17:20.740 --> 17:21.200
Exactly.

17:21.460 --> 17:24.380
We both authored our own future using the same constraint.

17:24.620 --> 17:26.360
But this brings up a huge problem.

17:26.360 --> 17:35.980
If a system is actively authoring its own interpretations, the original designer, whether that's an AI engineer in a lab or millions of years of evolution,

17:36.420 --> 17:39.240
completely loses absolute control over the outcome.

17:39.440 --> 17:42.760
Why on earth would evolution build a system that it can't perfectly control?

17:42.760 --> 17:45.920
That trade-off is the third major principle in the paper.

17:46.880 --> 17:48.540
Robustness is not control.

17:49.420 --> 17:54.920
When you build a system that interprets its own traces rather than just deferring to an external reader,

17:55.520 --> 17:59.420
the system's behavior stops being fully specifiable in advance.

18:00.320 --> 18:06.400
The engineer, or evolution, can no longer guarantee the exact pixel-perfect output.

18:06.400 --> 18:10.620
The price of self-interpreting memory is the death of micromanagement.

18:10.780 --> 18:11.200
Exactly.

18:11.620 --> 18:18.420
But what you get in return for giving up micromanagement is incredible robustness, resilience, and adaptive plasticity.

18:18.600 --> 18:20.280
So it shifts the dynamic entirely.

18:20.440 --> 18:26.320
It's the difference between typing code into a machine, where you demand and expect the exact same output every single time,

18:26.800 --> 18:29.360
versus negotiating with a highly intelligent animal.

18:29.480 --> 18:30.640
Oh, that's a good way to frame it.

18:30.720 --> 18:33.440
Think of a working dog, like a highly trained border collie.

18:33.440 --> 18:36.780
You give the collie a command to herd a flock of sheep into a pen.

18:37.280 --> 18:38.900
You know the dog will act coherently.

18:39.100 --> 18:41.600
It will accomplish the overarching goal based on its training.

18:41.980 --> 18:46.900
But you absolutely cannot guarantee exactly which path the dog will take across the field.

18:46.980 --> 18:48.480
Right, or which rock it will jump over.

18:48.720 --> 18:49.280
Exactly.

18:49.560 --> 18:53.640
Or how it will independently react if a wolf suddenly appears.

18:54.180 --> 18:56.700
You sacrifice absolute micromanagement,

18:56.700 --> 19:04.460
but you gain a system that can handle completely unpredictable, novel terrain that you never explicitly trained it for.

19:04.820 --> 19:06.480
And this isn't just a philosophical metaphor.

19:07.100 --> 19:11.840
This brings us to the most quantitative, rigorous piece of evidence discussed in the conversation,

19:12.520 --> 19:16.000
citing work from Federico Pagotzi, who was also at Levin's lab.

19:16.500 --> 19:20.780
They studied this exact trade-off using a concept called causal emergence.

19:21.240 --> 19:22.660
Okay, causal emergence.

19:22.660 --> 19:23.620
This sounds heavy.

19:23.620 --> 19:25.960
How do we break this down without losing the plot?

19:26.120 --> 19:27.200
What actually is it?

19:27.660 --> 19:31.720
At its core, causal emergence is a measurable mathematical quantity.

19:32.540 --> 19:37.260
It describes how much of a system's behavior is driven by its higher level whole system structure,

19:37.400 --> 19:41.340
rather than just being driven by the sun of its microscopic individual parts.

19:41.600 --> 19:44.440
So it's the forest acting as a unified forest,

19:44.560 --> 19:47.400
rather than just a bunch of individual trees doing their own thing.

19:47.500 --> 19:47.860
Exactly.

19:48.000 --> 19:49.620
It's a measure of top-down agency.

19:49.620 --> 19:54.100
When causal emergence is high, the macro state of the system is calling the shots,

19:54.480 --> 19:55.960
dictating what the microparts do.

19:56.660 --> 19:59.320
And Pagotzi's findings were absolutely remarkable.

19:59.920 --> 20:04.420
They looked at artificial neural networks and found that systems with high causal emergence

20:04.420 --> 20:06.960
are incredibly good at learning.

20:07.540 --> 20:12.620
But even more interestingly, the very act of training these systems, giving them experiences,

20:13.060 --> 20:15.680
increases their causal emergence even further.

20:16.540 --> 20:18.840
Learning builds up the system's structural agency.

20:18.840 --> 20:23.620
Okay, so you learn things, and your brain's architecture gets more integrated,

20:23.820 --> 20:24.660
more top-down.

20:25.060 --> 20:26.320
That makes intuitive sense.

20:26.620 --> 20:30.100
But here's the part of the paper that forced me to put it down and just stare at the wall.

20:30.400 --> 20:31.080
The memory wipe.

20:31.200 --> 20:31.520
Yes.

20:32.000 --> 20:34.700
The researchers took this system, they trained it,

20:34.820 --> 20:38.360
they watched its causal emergence literally rise on the graph,

20:38.500 --> 20:42.740
and then they forced the system to completely forget the specific content it just learned.

20:42.880 --> 20:44.540
They wiped the specific memories.

20:44.900 --> 20:45.940
And this is the punchline.

20:45.940 --> 20:50.300
When they wiped the memories, the gain in causal emergence did not reverse.

20:50.620 --> 20:51.480
It stayed high.

20:52.120 --> 20:57.000
Levin calls this an asymmetric ratchet, pointing upwards in terms of intelligence and agency.

20:57.260 --> 20:59.860
That is just staggeringly profound.

21:00.660 --> 21:01.720
So what does this all mean?

21:02.280 --> 21:07.340
The structural capacity to integrate information, the actual intelligence of the network,

21:07.920 --> 21:11.740
survives the erasure of the data that caused the change in the first place.

21:11.840 --> 21:12.700
Yeah, it's incredible.

21:12.700 --> 21:14.520
The memory outlives the storage.

21:15.200 --> 21:17.860
Just by having the experience of learning something,

21:18.300 --> 21:21.200
the architecture of the system permanently upgraded itself.

21:21.660 --> 21:26.380
And even when the specific facts were deleted, the structural upgrade remained.

21:26.600 --> 21:28.460
It's a one-way street pointing upward.

21:28.660 --> 21:29.880
It is profound.

21:30.260 --> 21:33.460
But Levin is incredibly careful here to make a crucial caveat.

21:33.460 --> 21:39.500
He stresses this isn't just some biological magic specific to living frog cells or human brains.

21:39.580 --> 21:41.060
That's not just a biology thing.

21:41.180 --> 21:44.420
No, this is a property of the mathematics of networks in general.

21:44.800 --> 21:51.280
They found this asymmetric ratchet even in purely random networks that have absolutely no evolutionary history whatsoever.

21:52.040 --> 21:56.820
It is a fundamental property of how complex networks process and integrate information over time.

21:56.820 --> 22:01.640
Which is the perfect conceptual bridge to how we actually solve the AI problem.

22:01.820 --> 22:08.840
Because the whole point of Flyxion's paper is arguing that we don't need to invent entirely new mystical branches of math

22:08.840 --> 22:11.740
to understand these complex biological phenomena.

22:11.880 --> 22:12.040
Right.

22:12.100 --> 22:12.980
We don't need magic.

22:13.160 --> 22:13.520
Exactly.

22:13.620 --> 22:15.500
We don't need to just say biology is magic.

22:15.620 --> 22:20.920
We just need to properly map these biological realities into an existing mathematical framework.

22:20.920 --> 22:26.100
And that framework is called the recursive continuation theorem and it's adaptation hierarchy.

22:26.620 --> 22:26.720
Right.

22:27.060 --> 22:34.980
If we want to understand why AI is stuck and biology isn't, we have to look at the three nested levels of this adaptation hierarchy.

22:35.460 --> 22:37.180
Let's systematically walk through them.

22:37.440 --> 22:39.000
And we won't get bogged down in the algebra.

22:39.260 --> 22:40.540
We'll focus on the mechanisms.

22:40.720 --> 22:41.140
Yes, please.

22:41.380 --> 22:42.540
Give me the functional reality.

22:42.860 --> 22:43.640
What is level one?

22:43.900 --> 22:45.760
Level one is called state modification.

22:46.180 --> 22:46.300
Yeah.

22:46.300 --> 22:52.980
Conceptually, this means you have a fixed rule and that fixed rule acts on a changing state under changing conditions.

22:53.420 --> 22:56.940
Think of it like playing a video game where the underlying physics engine is totally locked.

22:57.580 --> 22:58.800
Gravity is always gravity.

22:59.080 --> 23:00.860
The speed of light is always the speed of light.

23:01.320 --> 23:02.300
The game state changes.

23:02.460 --> 23:03.240
Your character moves.

23:03.360 --> 23:04.100
Enemies appear.

23:04.580 --> 23:08.140
But the rules governing how they move are absolutely fixed.

23:08.520 --> 23:08.800
Okay.

23:08.940 --> 23:10.760
So fixed rules meeting new conditions.

23:10.760 --> 23:21.380
If we map our earlier examples onto this, the developmental biology case, the frog genome and the antrobots, belongs right here in level one state modification.

23:22.040 --> 23:22.880
Let's see if that fits.

23:23.220 --> 23:23.420
Right.

23:23.500 --> 23:26.880
Because the genome, which acts as the operator of the rule, didn't mutate.

23:27.220 --> 23:27.820
It was fixed.

23:28.240 --> 23:31.740
But the context, the environment of the petri dish, changed.

23:31.740 --> 23:38.740
And that fixed rule operating in a new environment led to a totally different state, a swimming robot instead of a flat skin cell.

23:38.960 --> 23:39.260
Precisely.

23:39.420 --> 23:42.920
The rule was fixed, but the outcome varied radically based on the input.

23:43.580 --> 23:50.940
Now, there is a fascinating complication cited in the paper here from a thinker named Heisinger who wrote a book called History Before Function.

23:51.480 --> 23:53.380
He raises a very difficult question.

23:54.160 --> 23:57.580
Are the rule and the history actually separable in biology?

23:57.780 --> 23:58.840
What does that mean, separable?

23:58.840 --> 24:03.300
Well, in our video game analogy, the code for gravity is clearly separate from the character jumping.

24:04.160 --> 24:11.340
But Heisinger argues that in many biological structures, the rule and the history aren't two different things sitting in separate boxes.

24:11.780 --> 24:15.540
They are a single, inseparable, ongoing physical computation.

24:15.820 --> 24:17.960
Like the structure itself is the computation.

24:18.120 --> 24:18.580
Exactly.

24:18.980 --> 24:22.300
The protein folding is the history and the rule simultaneously.

24:22.300 --> 24:32.060
The Flyxion paper acknowledges this fuzziness and sets it aside for analytical convenience so we can use the math, but it's a vital piece of friction to keep in mind.

24:32.680 --> 24:36.080
Biology rarely has clean boundaries between the software and the hardware.

24:36.400 --> 24:37.060
That's a great caveat.

24:37.340 --> 24:40.480
Okay, so level one is a fixed rule changing the state.

24:40.760 --> 24:41.620
What is level two?

24:41.880 --> 24:43.400
Level two is parameter modification.

24:44.040 --> 24:47.120
Here, the mechanism itself is updated based on the state and the environment.

24:47.120 --> 25:00.620
Going back to our video game, imagine playing a game that watches how you play and actually rewrites its own core physics engine, literally changing the gravity while you are still holding the controller based on your play style.

25:00.960 --> 25:03.360
Ah, so the rule is no longer fixed.

25:03.680 --> 25:08.120
The system is rewriting its own rules based on its ongoing experience.

25:08.120 --> 25:13.180
The mechanism updating the rule is what the math calls the G function, right?

25:13.420 --> 25:15.060
Yes, the endogenous G function.

25:15.420 --> 25:20.440
This maps perfectly to the Pagotzi causal emergence case we just talked about.

25:20.740 --> 25:21.860
That belongs in level two.

25:22.440 --> 25:26.540
When a nervous system learns, it physically changes its synaptic organization.

25:27.100 --> 25:30.060
The learning process changes the physical mechanism itself.

25:30.560 --> 25:34.400
And as we saw with the asymmetric upward ratchet, this revision is permanent.

25:34.660 --> 25:37.560
It doesn't just symmetrically reverse when you forget the data.

25:37.560 --> 25:38.040
Exactly.

25:38.500 --> 25:42.420
You are permanently modifying the parameters of the system through the act of experiencing.

25:42.980 --> 25:46.040
And just for structural completeness, level three is rule modification.

25:46.800 --> 25:51.640
This is where the process that updates the mechanism that G function is itself subject to change.

25:51.960 --> 25:53.960
The system changes the way it changes its rules.

25:54.100 --> 25:57.100
So meta, that's like evolution modifying how evolution works.

25:57.220 --> 25:57.620
Pretty much.

25:57.840 --> 26:03.680
But for our purposes of comparing humans to AI, level one and level two are where the critical action is right now.

26:03.680 --> 26:11.980
Because now that we have this adaptation hierarchy, we can finally diagnose exactly, structurally, what is missing from current artificial intelligence.

26:12.300 --> 26:18.680
We can turn Katrina Schleisman's philosophical critique about episodic memory into a formal mathematical observation.

26:18.680 --> 26:22.920
And this is formalized as observation 5.1 in the paper.

26:23.240 --> 26:28.540
It diagnoses what we can call the training versus inference trap of modern large language models.

26:28.700 --> 26:31.900
Let's break this down because this is the absolute crux of the issue.

26:31.900 --> 26:37.480
When you interact with an LLM on your computer, you are interacting with it during its inference phase.

26:37.980 --> 26:39.460
The system has an active state.

26:40.060 --> 26:43.980
Its artificial neurons are firing as it calculates the next word in the chat with you.

26:44.440 --> 26:47.900
But crucially, it has absolutely no endogenous G function.

26:48.280 --> 26:51.540
It has no rule updating mechanism coupled to that live episode.

26:51.740 --> 26:59.480
So what you're saying is while I am talking to ChatGPT, it is impossible for ChatGPT to rewrite its own internal rules based on our conversation.

26:59.480 --> 27:10.960
Exactly. Any actual revision to the AI's core mechanisms, any change to its billions of synaptic weights, any fundamental learning, only happens during its initial training phase.

27:11.140 --> 27:16.380
And that training phase happened months ago in a massive server farm completely offline.

27:16.540 --> 27:17.200
Oh, wow.

27:17.300 --> 27:23.540
That training is 100% structurally decoupled from any real-time conversation it is having with you right now.

27:23.720 --> 27:27.380
It's just running a fixed level one state modification during the chat.

27:27.380 --> 27:33.440
If we connect this to the bigger picture, the reason AI lacks an episodic layer isn't just because it has a bad memory.

27:34.000 --> 27:41.500
It is the complete and total absence of rule modification machinery operating on the timescale of the system's lived experiences.

27:42.100 --> 27:47.200
It fundamentally cannot do level two parameter modification while it is interacting with the world.

27:47.560 --> 27:48.460
That is exactly it.

27:48.780 --> 27:51.800
Biology revises its operator during the episode of continuation.

27:51.800 --> 27:59.360
While you are living, while you are experiencing this deep dive right now, your physical rules, your synaptic weights are actively changing.

27:59.720 --> 28:02.060
Your causal emergence is ratcheting up in real time.

28:02.360 --> 28:10.060
AI, on the other hand, only revises itself between episodes during those massive, multi-million dollar offline training runs.

28:10.060 --> 28:13.660
This is precisely why AI feels so frozen in time.

28:14.220 --> 28:19.640
It is a brilliant, unbelievably massive semantic library, but it is statically frozen.

28:19.900 --> 28:23.060
It is a snapshot of the internet from whenever its training run ended.

28:23.720 --> 28:30.020
While biological organisms are continuously evolving and ratcheting upward in real time, the AI is stuck.

28:30.180 --> 28:37.140
It is the formal difference between retained admissibility, which biology possesses, and merely stored content, which AI possesses.

28:37.140 --> 28:43.480
Biology retains the structural capacity to act differently in the future based on the architectural changes built by the past.

28:44.060 --> 28:46.620
AI just looks up the past in a static database.

28:46.780 --> 28:49.180
What an unbelievably profound distinction.

28:49.600 --> 28:54.460
We've covered a staggering amount of ground today, moving from philosophy to biology to network math.

28:54.920 --> 28:57.220
Let's do a quick recap to lock this architecture in.

28:57.240 --> 28:57.600
Good idea.

28:57.600 --> 29:00.300
We learned that true memory isn't a hard drive.

29:00.580 --> 29:07.440
It's an active, creative interpretation of traces left by your past self, optimized for salience, not fidelity.

29:08.240 --> 29:16.820
We learned that historical constraints give us agency, narrowing our possibilities without determining our exact choices, much like walls guiding you down a hallway.

29:17.080 --> 29:17.200
Right.

29:17.200 --> 29:23.340
We saw that to gain incredible biological robustness, you have to trade away micromanagement and total control.

29:23.540 --> 29:25.120
You have to let the dog off the leash.

29:25.720 --> 29:38.900
And finally, we discovered that true intelligence, mapped mathematically, requires a system that alters its own operating rules in real time based on its lived experiences, something current AI is structurally walled off from doing.

29:38.900 --> 29:44.360
But the beauty of Flyxion's paper is that it doesn't just wrap everything up neatly and pretend we have all the answers.

29:44.620 --> 29:47.800
It actually leaves us with a grand unsolved mystery.

29:48.460 --> 29:52.700
At the very end of the text, the author poses open question 6.1.

29:53.100 --> 29:56.620
And this raises an incredibly important question for the future of intelligence.

29:56.840 --> 29:58.180
I love a good unsolved mystery.

29:58.300 --> 29:58.860
What is it?

29:59.280 --> 30:05.620
Under what conditions does the learning mechanism, that G process we talked about, inherit irreversibility from its history?

30:05.940 --> 30:08.000
Let's unpack that final provocation.

30:08.000 --> 30:14.800
We know from Pagazzi's causal emergent study that learning permanently changes the structure of a network.

30:15.340 --> 30:20.540
The structural gain, the intelligence upgrade, survives the erasure of the specific memories.

30:20.840 --> 30:22.480
It permanently ratchets upward.

30:22.760 --> 30:24.540
The massive provocation is this.

30:25.040 --> 30:30.800
Is this path asymmetry, this permanent upward ratchet of intelligence where memory functionally outlives storage,

30:31.440 --> 30:37.020
is it a universal mathematical law built into the very fabric of how the universe processes information?

30:37.020 --> 30:40.680
Or is it a special, exceptionally rare physical condition?

30:40.780 --> 30:43.040
Right, like maybe only biological things can do it.

30:43.120 --> 30:43.440
Exactly.

30:43.620 --> 30:51.780
And if it is rare, is it something we'll have to deliberately figure out how to engineer into future AI to make it truly capable of consciousness and lived experience?

30:52.140 --> 30:53.540
That is the ultimate frontier.

30:53.540 --> 30:58.120
Is the upward ratchet of intelligence a guarantee of complex networks?

30:58.340 --> 31:03.460
Or is it a biological miracle that we haven't even begun to figure out how to code yet?

31:04.340 --> 31:07.840
I want to leave you with this final thought, circling all the way back to where we started.

31:08.180 --> 31:14.700
The next time you find yourself frustrated because you forgot a specific fact or name or crucial detail from years ago,

31:14.700 --> 31:16.420
don't be hard on yourself.

31:16.620 --> 31:18.660
Stop thinking of your brain as a broken hard dive.

31:18.860 --> 31:20.620
Seriously, stop doing that.

31:20.980 --> 31:26.000
Consider that the very act of having learned it, of having lived that experience in the first place,

31:26.340 --> 31:29.540
may have permanently upgraded the physical architecture of your mind.

31:29.860 --> 31:35.040
The specific data might be gone, but the structural gain, the intelligence, remains.

31:35.040 --> 31:38.520
Thank you for joining this deep dive into the source material.

31:38.940 --> 31:39.580
See you next time.

